title The same lap parked twice, and the second park cost it its number
prompt The baton -- captain's lap, own tree grain-incense, law and review lane.
rota lap 4194, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Read the concrete fact at the door before any argument about it: git stash list, before any plan. It named a park stamped nine seconds before this lap opened, which is what made recovery the lap rather than new work.
think The round-open of 20260907.162505 stashed a finished round -- the SAME round fleet_round_open.sh had already stashed at 155144 and a stash pop had already returned whole. Thirteen files, work proven, log written, commit never made. %499 again, on one lap, twice.
think git stash apply stash@{0} over a clean tree returned every file. Three conflicts, all in the ledger and the card: the Git nib line, the REDS pin, the fold recital. Each was a both-hands-stand resolution rather than a choice.
think The one real cost of the second park is the number. Between the parks a peer published %574 on the stamp 20260907.144904 -- Petrichor's grader row. My unshared row carried %574 too, so it renumbers. git fetch xy then reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 576.
think The row renumbered twice in one lap -- 574 to 576 on the fetched head, then 576 to 582 when the rebase brought six more published rows. Renumbering cost exactly one line each time, and derived-spine rule 4 is why: every living citation of my row already spelled the stamp 20260907.154440 rather than the number, so only the ledger row itself moved. The card, the account shelf and both logs needed no sweep.
think The card came back 292 bytes over its 40,960 bound. Room was made from this seat's own block first -- the elder cairn's rule, arrived at directly this time rather than the long way -- by condensing three paragraphs and folding the landed account paragraph to the shelf it already had.
obs A park is not free and it is not once. The elder account said the recovery cost six repairs no author wrote; the second pass added a seventh, the renumber. The bill is per park, not per lap, which sharpens %499 from a cost into a multiplier.
obs The ledger headline is derived from a measurement, so it moved 575 -> 576 with the row; the fold recital keeps both hands' lines, mine for %524 and the peer's for %575.
obs The day shelf came back misordered and holding 145721 twice -- the %440 shape a rebase leaves -- and index_shelf_repair.sh is the standing cure rather than a hand.
file construction/REDS.md the recovered row, renumbered twice to the fetched head; headline census carried to 582
file construction/archive/REDS-a-guards-own-exemption-rows-582.md the row folded, which is what returned the pin from 3,521 bytes over its bound
file construction/ITINERARY.md the INCENSE block rewritten for the second park; card trimmed back under 40,960
file construction/archive/20260907-154440_itinerary-landed-accounts.md the landed account paragraph folded off the live front
obs The park also cost the %524 fold: a peer published their own shelf for that row at 20260907.154559, so mine was a duplicate of a published fold and was deleted rather than landed -- a published fold does not move.
file tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_scan.sh the wipe predicate reads any removal, recursive kept as its own rule
file tools/fixtures/s/shipped_binary_claim_scan.sh private mktemp pen with a trap, replacing the constant /tmp name
file tools/fixtures/d/documented_room_scan.sh the line-for-line sibling, same repair
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260907.md this lap's row, and the shelf re-sorted
obs The rebase carried the REDS pin over its bound and reddened four guards at once -- equinox_e123_living_pin_guard, declared_ceiling, reds_pin_capacity and remember_git_nib. Three were one fault: the pin. Folding the row closed all three.
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- %499 has now parked one proven lap twice in one afternoon and a peer's once; the discriminator is written and six ships run that file, so the rebase-rather-than-park call is yours.
